WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.458
One gigabyte of

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:02.590
Wikipedia squeezed

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.477
into 105 megabytes

00:00:04.998 --> 00:00:06.413
Tufa Labs' tufazip

00:00:06.224 --> 00:00:07.533
losslessly compressed

00:00:07.345 --> 00:00:08.523
one billion bytes

00:00:08.335 --> 00:00:09.443
of Wikipedia to

00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:11.660
105.39 megabytes

00:00:11.779 --> 00:00:12.957
including its weights

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:13.759
and decoder

00:00:14.278 --> 00:00:15.233
It took second

00:00:15.045 --> 00:00:15.858
place on Matt

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.072
Mahoney's Large Text

00:00:16.884 --> 00:00:18.110
Compression Benchmark

00:00:18.311 --> 00:00:19.313
the best entry

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.068
using a neural

00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:20.858
network as its

00:00:20.811 --> 00:00:21.836
only predictor

00:00:21.976 --> 00:00:23.037
Everything above it

00:00:22.848 --> 00:00:24.298
mixes a transformer

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.513
with hand-built models

00:00:26.054 --> 00:00:28.530
A 163-million-parameter

00:00:28.342 --> 00:00:30.075
Transformer keeps learning

00:00:29.887 --> 00:00:30.995
while it compresses

00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:32.127
and the decoder

00:00:31.938 --> 00:00:33.129
repeats that learning

00:00:33.295 --> 00:00:34.462
so learned weights

00:00:34.273 --> 00:00:35.216
never ship

00:00:35.742 --> 00:00:36.756
Full story on

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:37.463
the dashboard
